## 3. Probe the realtime session
```
beam_exec("listen player --probe -q")
```
Signs in a guest, exchanges its token for the realtime JWT, connects to the realm's socket with `send-session-start=true`, sends the `session-start` frame and reports the handshake status, time to open and frames received. It exits non-zero when the socket can't open or the server closes it. This is the quickest way to tell "the realm is misconfigured" from "this network blocks WebSockets".

## 4. Read deployed logs
```
beam_exec("project remote-logs MatchService -q")
```
Filter with `--filter <text>`, `--server-log-level <level>`, `--from`/`--to`. Use `deploy ps` to see whether each service is running and current.

## 5. Inspect storage
- Locally, with the storage running (`project run`): `beam_exec("project open-mongo MatchStorage -q")` opens Mongo Express.
- Save a local storage's data: `beam_exec("project storage snapshot MatchStorage -q")`.

## 6. Browser smoke test
Run a real browser against the built site and fail unless the realtime socket opens, the `session-start` frame is sent and a gameplay call succeeds. A Playwright script (adapted from `web/e2e/smoke.mjs` in https://github.com/ali-beamable/TestGameBeamable):
```js
import { chromium } from "playwright";

const BASE_URL = process.env.BASE_URL ?? "http://localhost:4173/";
const browser = await chromium.launch();
const page = await browser.newPage();

const socket = { opened: false, sessionStartSent: false, closed: false };
page.on("websocket", (ws) => {
  if (!ws.url().includes("/connect")) return;
  ws.on("framesent", (f) => {
    socket.opened = true;
    if (String(f.payload).includes('"session-start"')) socket.sessionStartSent = true;
  });
  ws.on("framereceived", () => (socket.opened = true));
  ws.on("close", () => (socket.closed = true));
});

let ok = false;
try {
  await page.goto(BASE_URL);
  await page.waitForSelector("#board:not([hidden])", { timeout: 45000 }); // your "Beam.init resolved" marker
  if (!socket.opened || !socket.sessionStartSent) throw new Error("realtime socket did not open / no session-start frame");

  const played = page.waitForResponse((r) => r.url().includes("/PlayRound"), { timeout: 15000 });
  await page.click("#moves button[data-move=rock]");
  const response = await played;
  if (!response.ok()) throw new Error(`PlayRound failed: HTTP ${response.status()}`);
  if (socket.closed) throw new Error("realtime socket closed during the session");
  ok = true;
} finally {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ ok, socket }));
  await browser.close();
}
process.exit(ok ? 0 : 1);
```
Serve the build with `npm run build && npx vite preview --port 4173` and run it with `node e2e/smoke.mjs`.

## Sandboxes and proxies break WebSockets
Agent sandboxes and TLS-intercepting proxies often let HTTP through but block or break browser WebSockets, so `Beam.init` times out even though every REST call works. When that happens:
- Verify realtime with `listen player --probe` and from CI (e.g. a GitHub Actions job on an open network running the smoke test), not only from the sandbox.
- **Never stub or mock the WebSocket in a test that claims to verify sessions.** A stubbed socket proves nothing about the realm.

